**Read:** [Guideline for Safe Practices for Cryogenic Air Separation Plants — EIGA 147/22](../sources/Cryogenic-Air-Separation-EIGA.lingua#r_39BST0TM9XV9VEZ14NXVJW7TWA), operation/training chapters; [Static vacuum insulated cryogenic vessels — EIGA 224/20](../sources/Static-Cryogenic-Vessels-EIGA.lingua#r_3RWNED7WZ5GMG7X76ZT96A63HZ), operation and inspection; [Fundamentals of Vacuum Technology — Walter Umrath and contributors](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Vacuum-Technology-Leybold.lingua#r_1S93A5X32978GSHZH4CF96VPQR), the chapters appropriate to the selected pumps.

Idle, warm standby, cooldown, production, regeneration, maintenance and recovery are physically different states. A boundary that contains pure gas during steady use may contain air after access. A warm line, cold receiver and stopped pump may coexist; naming the whole installation “off” hides those differences.

For every state, describe allowed material connections, energy sources, inventories and the observations that establish the state. A transition has a duration, intermediate states and possible interruptions. The stable endpoint does not establish that the route to it is acceptable.

**Work:** Draw a state diagram for a published system and attach mass/energy accounts to two transitions. Interrupt a transition halfway in the model. Identify the retained inventory and evidence needed to determine the resulting state without assuming that the previous command completed.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
